readings.c3s1:
¶
'''adrift''' (ạ-drift′), ''prep. phr.'' as ''adv.'' or ''a.'' [< ''a''<sup>3</sup> + ''drift.''] '''1.''' Floating at random; not fastened by any kind of moorings; at the mercy of winds and currents.
{{smaller block|<poem>Trees ''adrift''
Down the great river.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., xi. 832.}}
<poem>So on the sea she shall be set ''adrift'',
And who relieves her dies.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', Marriage à la Mode, iii.}}}}
Hence—'''2.''' Figuratively, swayed by any chance impulse; all abroad; at a loss.
{{smaller block|Frequent reflection will keep their minds from running ''adrift.''
{{right|''Locke'', Education.}}}}

{{smaller|'''To turn adrift''', to unmoor; set drifting; hence, figuratively, to turn away, dismiss, or discharge, as from home, employment, etc.; throw upon the world.}}
{{smaller block|Great multitudes who had been employed in the woollen manufactories, or in the mines, were ''turned adrift.''
{{right|''Lecky'', Eng. in 18th Cent., i.}}}}

'''adrip''' (ạ-drip′), ''prep. phr.'' as ''adv.'' or ''a.'' [< ''a''<sup>3</sup> + ''drip.''] In a dripping state. ''D. G. Mitchell.''

'''adrogate''' (ad′rọ̄-gāt), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''adrogated'', ppr. ''adrogating.'' [< L. ''adrogatus'', pp. of ''adrogare'', later ''arrogare'', take a ''homo sui juris'' (a person not under the power of his father) in the place of a child, adopt, < ''ad'', to, + ''rogare'', ask. The same word in other senses gave rise to ''arrogate'', q. v. See ''adrogation.''] To adopt by adrogation.
{{smaller block|Clodius, the enemy of Cicero, was ''adrogated'' into a plebeian family.
{{right|''Smith'', Dict. Antiq., p. 15.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
{{smaller block|tact. ''Expert'' emphasizes experience, practice, and hence is commonly a lower word than ''skilful'', which makes knowledge the principal thing: a ''skilful'' mechanic makes more use of his mind than an ''expert'' mechanic. ''Clever'' implies notable quickness, readiness, resource in practical affairs, and sometimes the lack of the larger powers of mind: a ''clever'' mechanic has fertility in planning and skill in executing what is planned. A ''clever'' statesman may or may not be an able one; a man may be ''clever'' in evil.}}

{{smaller block|Why, says Plato, if he be manually so ''adroit'', likely he will turn pickpocket. {{right|''S. Lanier'', The Eng. Novel, p. 117.}}}}

{{smaller block|The ''dexterous'' management of terms, and being able to fend and prove with them, passes for a great part of learning. {{right|''Locke.''}}}}

{{smaller block|His only books were an almanac and an arithmetic, in which last he was considerably ''expert''.
{{right|''Thoreau'', Walden, p. 161.}}}}

{{smaller block|Thus, like a ''skilful'' chess-player, by little and little he draws out his men, and makes his pawns of use to his greater persons. {{right|''Dryden'', Dram. Poesy.}}}}

{{smaller block|But the names of the ''clever'' men who invented canoes and bows and arrows are as utterly unknown to tradition as the names of the earliest myth-makers.
{{right|''J. Fiske'', Evolutionist, p. 204.}}}}

'''adroitly''' (ạ-droit′li), ''adv.'' In an adroit manner; with dexterity; readily; skilfully.

{{smaller block|He [Eadmund] turned his new conquest ''adroitly'' to account by using it to bind to himself the most dangerous among his foes. {{right|''J. R. Green'', Conq. of Eng., p. 266.}}}}

'''adroitness''' (ạ-droit′nes), ''n.'' The quality of being adroit; dexterity; readiness in the use of the hands or of the mental faculties.

{{smaller block|Sir John Blaquire had some debating power and great skill and ''adroitness'' in managing men.
{{right|''Lecky'', Eng. in 18th Cent., xvi.}}}}

'''adroop''' (ạ-dröp′), ''prep. phr.'' as ''adv.'' [< ''a''<sup>3</sup> + ''droop''.] In a drooping position. ''J. D. Long'', Æneid, xi. 1128.
